It's not that.  It's that USENET is a good conversational/discussion medium, and
generally a lousy brainstorming medium.

The problem with USENET (or email, or a mailing list, etc.) is that it allows 
someone to begin from an inaccurate presumption and build an entire airy
webwork pyramid of idea, all built upon the inaccurate presumption...
before anyone ever sees it.  Then they post it.

Then you come along, poke at the inaccurate presumption, and ignore the
rest of the post, and people are horrified because you didn't RESPOND TO
THEM, so they step onto the "Why do you hate (idea)" soapbox and lead
off... without understanding that it's not that I (or anyone else) hates
(idea), it's that you got to (idea) in a bad way.

This isn't remotely confined to Nethack.  I've been on mailing lists and
other fora (including USENET) where game changes/design were discussed, and 
_the worst possible_ way to get new things discussed was over USENET.

IRC (or any other real-time conversational medium) allows you to step in after 
the inaccurate presumption and discuss _that_; at which point the
original ideamaker isn't offended because you ignored the rest of his
idea, nor does he think you're against the idea, and it all goes much
easier.  And perhaps (idea) happens anyway, just got there off of good
presumptions.

